      <span class="tag tag--emerald">Hiring Help</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 16px 0 14px;">I've been burned by tech VAs before: here's how to find the right one</h1>

      <div class="article-meta-row">
        <span class="avatar" style="background-image:url('/images/about.jpg')"></span>
        <span>By Gee</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Oct 5, 2026</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
      </div>

      <div class="article-hero-img" style="background-image:url('/images/blog-ghl.jpg')"></div>

      <div class="toc">
        <h3>What's inside</h3>
        <a href="#what-went-wrong">What usually went wrong (it's rarely the tech)</a>
        <a href="#who-owns-it">Who owns the accounts</a>
        <a href="#what-done-means">What "done" actually means</a>
        <a href="#when-it-breaks">What happens when something breaks</a>
        <a href="#plain-words">Can they explain it in plain words</a>
        <a href="#after-handoff">What you're left with at the end</a>
      </div>

      <p>If you've been burned by a tech person before, hiring the next one probably feels like handing your house keys to a stranger and hoping. Honestly, that caution is fair. You still need the help, the mess behind the scenes isn't going to fix itself, but you can't afford to go through that again.</p>
      <p>So here's what I'd look at before hiring anyone, me included. None of it needs you to understand the tech. It's all about the stuff around the tech, which is where most of these stories actually go wrong.</p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <h3>The quick version</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>When a tech hire goes badly, it's usually not a skill problem. It's access, scope, communication, or handoff that nobody sorted out up front.</li>
          <li>You don't need to judge their technical ability. You need to ask five plain questions and notice how clearly they answer.</li>
          <li>The right person makes you feel less in the dark with every conversation, not more.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="what-went-wrong">What usually went wrong (it's rarely the tech)</h2>
      <p>When someone tells me about a tech person who let them down, the story is almost never "they couldn't build it." It's that nobody wrote down what the finished thing was supposed to be. Or that things went quiet the first time something broke. Or that when the relationship ended, the logins and the platform account were in their name and not hers.</p>
      <p>That matters, because it changes what you screen for. If you only ask "are you good at GoHighLevel," you'll get a yes from everyone. The better question is how they work when things get messy, because things always get a little messy.</p>

      <h2 id="who-owns-it">Who owns the accounts</h2>
      <p>This one first, because it's the one you can't undo easily. Your domain, your GHL account or sub-account, your email platform, your payment processor, your calendar. Every one of those should be in your name, with the person you hire added as a team member or collaborator.</p>
      <p>If someone suggests setting things up under their own account "to make it easier" and moving it to you later, slow down. Sometimes that's innocent. It also means your business is sitting on their login.</p>
      <p class="check"><strong>Worth checking:</strong> for each tool in your business, can you name whose email address is the account owner? If you can't answer that for even one of them, that's worth fixing before you hire anyone.</p>

      <h2 id="what-done-means">What "done" actually means</h2>
      <p>"Set up my automations" can mean three very different things to two different people. Before any work starts, you should both be able to point at a written list of what's being built, what it should do, and what counts as finished.</p>
      <p>It doesn't need to be fancy. A shared doc is fine. What matters is that it exists, so when you get to the end and something feels off, you're comparing it to a page and not to a memory of a call.</p>
      <p>A good sign is when they ask you questions before quoting. A bad sign is a number with no questions, because that usually means the scope is going to get negotiated after the money has moved.</p>

      <h2 id="when-it-breaks">What happens when something breaks</h2>
      <p>Something will break. A form stops sending, an automation fires twice, a platform changes a setting overnight. That isn't the red flag. The red flag is not knowing what happens next.</p>
      <p>Ask directly: if something stops working on a Friday evening, what do I do and who do I tell? You're not looking for a promise of instant fixes. You're looking for a clear answer, how they want to be contacted, and roughly how fast they respond. A person who has thought about this has an answer ready. A person who hasn't will give you a vague "oh, just message me."</p>
      <p class="check"><strong>Worth checking:</strong> ask them how they handled the last thing that broke on someone else's setup. You don't need names. You want to hear whether they tell you what happened, or only what the fix was.</p>

      <h2 id="plain-words">Can they explain it in plain words</h2>
      <p>This is the easiest test and the most reliable one. Ask them to tell you what they're going to build, in the words you'd use, before they build it. If you finish the conversation understanding your own system better than you did before, that's a good sign.</p>
      <p>If you come away feeling like you needed a translator, or that asking another question would make you look silly, pay attention to that feeling. A person who can't explain it simply to the client usually can't document it simply for the next person either.</p>

      <h2 id="after-handoff">What you're left with at the end</h2>
      <p>The last thing to ask about is the part that gets skipped most. When the project is finished, what do you actually have? You should be able to see the list of what was built, where it lives, and how to change the simple stuff without having to message someone.</p>
      <p>Short written notes or a quick screen recording walking through it are plenty. The goal is that the system works because it was built properly, not because one person remembers how it fits together.</p>

      <h2>If you've been burned, start smaller</h2>
      <p>You don't have to hand over everything at once. A small, defined first piece, one automation, one fix, one cleanup, with the accounts in your name and the scope written down, tells you more about working with someone than any call will. If it goes well, you extend it. If it doesn't, you've lost a week and not a quarter.</p>
      <p>And if you'd rather have someone look at what you have first, before you hand it to anyone new, that's exactly what the audit is for.</p>

